GUI modernization, Phase 2 (partial - fonts, token contrast, first
components): vendored fonts, a real WCAG contrast pass over the design
tokens, and a generic Button/TechnicalValue component pair wired into six
files, closing P1-05 and part of P2/§4.9.

## 1. Fonts vendored (P1-05)

15 WOFF2 files (440KB), fetched directly from Google Fonts' CDN with one
HTTP request per family+weight - batching several weights in one request
(`family:wght@400;600;700`) was tried first and turned out to make the API
return three declarations pointing at the *same* file, caught by comparing
SHA-256 hashes across what should have been three different weights, not
by inspection. One request per weight reliably returns distinct files,
verified the same way. Full provenance (source, gfonts API version,
SHA-256 per file): `desktop/src/assets/fonts/README.md`. Licenses (SIL OFL
1.1 for all four families) fetched as the canonical `OFL.txt` from
`google/fonts`, not retyped from memory; entries added to `NOTICE` (which
listed no third-party licenses before this) and the desktop SBOM was
regenerated to reflect the new devDependencies.

Confirmed actually loading in a live browser session, not just declared:

    document.fonts (after document.fonts.ready): 9 entries with
    status "loaded", including "IBM Plex Sans Arabic 600", "Alexandria 700",
    "Inter 700" - real fetched/parsed font files, not silent fallback.

    getComputedStyle('.app-name').fontFamily
      = 'Alexandria, "IBM Plex Sans Arabic", Inter, "IBM Plex Sans Arabic", system-ui, sans-serif'
    getComputedStyle('.nav-item').fontFamily
      = '"IBM Plex Sans Arabic", Inter, "IBM Plex Sans Arabic", system-ui, sans-serif'

i.e. headings (`.app-name`/`.page-title`/`.incident-title`) get Alexandria
first in Arabic mode, body text does not - the cascade behaves exactly as
designed, not just as declared.

## 2. WCAG contrast pass over :root tokens - two real failures found

Wrote a small contrast-ratio script (WCAG relative-luminance formula, not a
library) and ran it over every foreground/background pair actually used in
`App.css`, light and dark:

**Failure 1** (light mode): `--warn-line` (#b3541e) on `--warn-bg` (#fbeee4)
= 4.39:1, needs 4.5:1 for the source banner's 0.88rem warning text - the
banner shown on literally every page in demo mode ("Demo mode - a real
recording..."). Darkened to `#a04618` (5.45:1). Same hue, comfortable
margin, re-verified visually (screenshot below) that it still reads as the
same warning amber.

**Failure 2** (dark mode, found only once real buttons existed to check):
`--accent` (#5fae86) and `--error` (#d4695f) are chosen to work as *text*
against dark backgrounds (and do - 6.09:1 and 4.60:1 there), but
`.btn-primary`/`.btn-danger` use them as a *solid fill with white text on
top*, a different constraint: white on #5fae86 measured 2.66:1, white on
#d4695f measured 3.51:1 - both fail WCAG AA's 4.5:1 for the button's
0.9rem/normal-weight label. Fixed with new `--accent-solid`/
`--danger-solid` tokens, held constant across themes (#2f6f4e / #a3312a -
the light-mode values, which already pass 5.99:1 / 6.93:1 with white text)
rather than compromising the text-color job `--accent`/`--error` already do
correctly in both themes.

