Not yet checked: WCAG 1.4.11 non-text/UI-component-boundary contrast (a
button's fill against its adjacent page background, as opposed to the text
on top of it) - `--accent-solid` against the dark page background measures
2.97:1, just under the 3:1 this separate success criterion asks for. Not
fixed in this pass: axe's default `color-contrast` rule checks text
contrast only and did not flag it, and a fix here trades off against the
white-text fix above along the same hue (verified by search - no color on
this hue satisfies both constraints past roughly this point). Tracked for
Phase 2's remaining work: a border color for solid buttons, independent of
the fill, is the standard way WCAG itself allows meeting 1.4.11 without
reopening the text-contrast question already closed correctly.

## 3. Two more real defects found while building Button/TechnicalValue,
neither in the execution order by name

**CSS specificity bug** (found by axe, `color-contrast`, serious): `.btn:hover
{ background: var(--sidebar-bg) }` (specificity: one class + one
pseudo-class) is MORE specific than plain `.btn-primary { background:
var(--accent) }` (one class) - so on hover, before this fix, *every*
button variant's background silently reverted to the generic hover
background regardless of variant, because `.btn-primary:hover` only
changed `opacity`, never re-asserting `background` to reclaim it. Diagnosed
by dumping the real computed style: at wizard steps 3-4 the "Next" button's
background measured `rgb(239, 238, 233)` (exactly `--sidebar-bg`) while
hovered, with white text on top - 1.15:1. Not visible in a quick look
because the button only occupies this state while the mouse is physically
over it, which the earlier manual screenshot review did not happen to
catch (mouse was elsewhere) - axe caught it because Playwright's `.click()`
leaves the cursor at the click position, and the wizard's Next button sits
at the same screen coordinates across steps, so a fresh render at that spot
inherits `:hover` from the previous step's click. Fixed with
`.btn:where(:hover)` - `:where()` contributes zero to specificity, so the
generic hover rule now ties with (and loses to, by source order) each
variant's own rules instead of always winning on `background`.

**Keyboard-inaccessible scroll regions** (axe, `scrollable-region-focusable`,
serious): every scroll container Phase 1 introduced
(`.workspace-body`, `.sidebar-nav`, `.wizard-step-body`) was a plain `<div
overflow-y: auto>` with no way to receive keyboard focus - a keyboard-only
user had no way to scroll any of them. Fixed with `tabIndex={0}` on all
three (`App.tsx`, `Sidebar.tsx`, `Wizard.tsx`) plus a `:focus-visible`
outline so the resulting focus state is actually visible, not just
technically present.

Both were caught by widening the axe suite past the narrow `.sidebar`-only
scope Phase 1 shipped with - a real example of the "don't stop at the
first passing test" instruction paying off: the Phase 1 suite was green
and not lying, it was just not looking at the two things this phase's own
new code broke.

